WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.940
A newborn killing nurse, a marble eating&nbsp;
mother, and a pop star whose fame is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.860
thanks to a school shooting. You'll never&nbsp;
think of "disturbing" movies the same way.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:16.620
The 2012 remake of Maniac had some mighty big&nbsp;
shoes to fill. The 1980 original is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.240
most renowned and reviled slashers of all&nbsp;
time. It's an appallingly intimate portrait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.220
of a serial killer who stalks the streets of&nbsp;
New York City in search of vulnerable women to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:32.160
work his mommy issues out on. Cut to 2012, when&nbsp;
the remake dared to ask: what if this already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.260
skin-crawling story was shot almost entirely from&nbsp;
the first-person point of view of the killer?

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:42.660
Working against type, Elijah Wood plays&nbsp;
Frank, a deeply disturbed "nice guy" who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.680
can't be reminded of his lack of a girlfriend&nbsp;
without killing every woman in the vicinity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.300
While Frank makes an admirable attempt&nbsp;
to date normally, he still has a bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.060
habit of draping women's scalps over vintage&nbsp;
mannequins. The result is a film that succeeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.520
in making Frank pathetic without also making&nbsp;
him worthy of our pity. And in the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.540
with the threat of violence perpetuated&nbsp;
by the rise of incels, the horrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.520
plausibility of Maniac has only become more&nbsp;
and more disturbing all these years later.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.420
2012's Excision follows a troubled young teen and&nbsp;
aspiring surgeon named Pauline. As her hormones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:24.780
rage, her corporeal interest proves far more&nbsp;
carnal than clinical. Obsessed and outcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.120
Pauline sinks deeper and deeper into her&nbsp;
blood-soaked fantasy life, where she dons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.400
scrubs and bathes in the blood of patients&nbsp;
who are as much her victims as her lovers.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.620
Wearing its depravity on its sleeve,&nbsp;
Excision dives deep into unapologetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.340
teenage boundary-pushing with a pitch&nbsp;
black wit and a scalpel in hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:51.180
The film has no shortage of gore, but its&nbsp;
true disturbing power lies in its portrait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:57.420
of a neglected, ostracized teen. Pauline's steady&nbsp;
slip into destruction has a clear and preventable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.480
trajectory, so it's agonizing to watch the world&nbsp;
fail her at every turn. A coming-of-age horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:09.000
that fully embraces all the bloody realities of&nbsp;
morbid teenage angst, Excision is a fiendishly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.740
offensive horror comedy about wanting to be&nbsp;
loved and accepted for the little freak you are.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.040
"Can you contract an STD from&nbsp;
having sex with a dead person?"

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.400
On the grounds of a vague religious disagreement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.660
Thomasin and her family are banished&nbsp;
from society. They build their cabin on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.740
the fringes of colonial New England, right&nbsp;
at the edge of a dark, forbidding forest.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:33.780
One afternoon, Thomasin's baby brother vanishes&nbsp;
without a trace. Suspicious of Thomasin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.920
the family reasons that the infant&nbsp;
was spirited away by a witch with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.880
dark intentions. As the days tick&nbsp;
by and the child remains missing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.680
a devious combination of isolation and&nbsp;
paranoia turn Thomasin's family against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:52.500
her. As they descend into madness, it becomes&nbsp;
increasingly difficult to parse cabin fever from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.240
the rumblings of an ever-encroaching force&nbsp;
that seems to be seeping out of the woods.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.700
One of the key factors in the dread-soaked&nbsp;
atmosphere of The Witch is its adherence to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.180
historical accuracy, making the&nbsp;
whole experience that much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.560
immersive. So when the supernatural begins&nbsp;
its infiltration in the family's homestead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.920
their struggle with unseen forces begins to feel&nbsp;
very real and threatening. Furthermore, the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:22.320
also invites you to see things from Thomasin's&nbsp;
perspective. She stands accused of unthinkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.780
acts. And the self-fulfilling prophecy of the&nbsp;
accusation is truly the stuff of nightmares.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.142
"Baa, baa, baa, baa."

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:37.080
Run, don't walk, as far away as you can from We&nbsp;
Are the Flesh! Or if you insist on toughing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:42.300
out, don't say we didn't warn you. Released in&nbsp;
2016, We Are the Flesh follows brother-sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:48.360
duo Lucio and Fauna, who are doing their best to&nbsp;
survive in a post-apocalyptic hellscape. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:53.700
then shocked when they enter a decrepit building&nbsp;
on the edge of town and find a man named Mariano.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:58.740
Ignoring the fire in his eyes, the siblings&nbsp;
listen attentively to Mariano's devil's deal:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:03.720
food and shelter in exchange for their help&nbsp;
in transforming a building into a cavernous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:10.140
womb-like temple to depravity itself. With Lucio&nbsp;
and Fauna firmly under his thumb, Mariano forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:15.480
the siblings to participate in unthinkable&nbsp;
acts of violence and fleshy transgression.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:23.160
The end result is a combination of sexual abandon&nbsp;
and hallucinatory terror. We Are the Flesh is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:30.600
intense, explicit, and boundary-defying descent&nbsp;
into madness. Is it daring, or is it disgusting?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.500
"Why not both?", seems to be&nbsp;
the answer of everyone involved.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.260
It's relatively bloodless compared to the gorier&nbsp;
entries on this list, but The Killing of a Sacred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.900
Deer is nevertheless a deeply unsettling viewing&nbsp;
experience. The film is a loose adaptation of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:51.600
of the most harrowing chapters in Ancient Greek&nbsp;
fiction, Iphigenia at Aulis, which tells of King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:58.260
Agamemnon's ritual sacrifice of his own daughter&nbsp;
in exchange for favorable winds to sail to Troy.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.600
Directed by Greek filmmaker Yorgos Lanthimos,&nbsp;
the film follows Dr. Steven Murphy, a successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.580
heart surgeon who appears to have it all. Then&nbsp;
one day, he meets a young man named Martin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:14.100
and the two form a special bond. It's only after&nbsp;
Martin inserts himself into the surgeon's family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:20.040
life that he reveals his true intentions:&nbsp;
he wants justice. Martin's father died on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.420
the operating table because Dr. Murphy&nbsp;
was under the influence. By supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:29.100
means that are never clearly explained&nbsp;
and are all the more disturbing for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.500
the surgeon's two children fall ill. Dr.&nbsp;
Murphy is faced with an impossible decision:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.220
he must kill one of his kids or&nbsp;
his wife, or they will all perish.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:44.426
"It's the only thing I can think&nbsp;
of that's close to justice."

00:05:44.426 --> 00:05:45.240
Much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:50.160
the uncomfortable atmosphere of The Killing of a&nbsp;
Sacred Deer comes from Lanthimos' intentionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.480
stilted direction. This endows everything&nbsp;
from the production design to the visceral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:00.480
moments of violence with an unnaturally&nbsp;
clinical distance. And the creep factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.900
of Barry Keoghan's performance as Martin cannot&nbsp;
be overstated. From his candor to his voracious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:13.020
appetite for spaghetti, everything he does&nbsp;
feels slimy and calculated. The thought of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:17.760
innocent lives being caught in the teeth of&nbsp;
life's meat grinder is inherently upsetting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.300
and The Killing of a Sacred Deer&nbsp;
makes an absolute meal out of it.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.160
The death of children is a running theme&nbsp;
on this list, and understandably so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.960
which is why we've got this film about a Satanic&nbsp;
cult hellbent on snuffing out as many innocent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.660
lives as possible. 2017's Belzebuth kicks&nbsp;
things off with a possessed nurse massacring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.520
a nursery full of shrieking newborns, and&nbsp;
it doesn't get much better from there.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.860
The young age of the film's victims will&nbsp;
obviously be a major turn-off to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:52.080
viewers. But if you can get past the initial&nbsp;
shock of the back-to-back sadistic set pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.820
you'll be delighted to find a sharp-edged&nbsp;
supernatural procedural that pulls no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:01.200
punches. Few films are this committed to&nbsp;
throwing the baby out with the bathwater.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.480
The main character is Emmanuel Ritter,&nbsp;
a dedicated, gravel-voiced detective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.280
with the physical presence of a bull and&nbsp;
the constitution to match. Still reeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.240
from the tragic death of his newborn son,&nbsp;
Detective Ritter finds himself embroiled in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:19.440
an investigation into the Catholic conspiracy&nbsp;
responsible for the mass murder of countless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.300
children. An ancient evil is dead-set&nbsp;
on clawing its way back into our world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:29.880
and the question of whether an emotionally shaken&nbsp;
detective and a would-be priest turned paranormal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:34.800
investigator are the right men for the job&nbsp;
keeps the stakes high and palpable throughout.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:41.580
If you've yet to come across Possum in your&nbsp;
moviegoing adventures, we're about to ruin your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:47.040
day. There are a number of reasons why this film&nbsp;
will get under your skin: the oppressive mood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.860
the enigmatic storytelling, and the enormous,&nbsp;
human-faced, spider-legged puppet monster.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.120
The plot follows Phillip, a disgraced&nbsp;
children's puppeteer who returns to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.200
hometown after losing his job for an&nbsp;
ominously unrevealed reason. While he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.920
attempts to destroy his grotesque&nbsp;
marionette, he comes face to face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:09.120
with his abusive uncle and the dark trauma&nbsp;
that has loomed over him his entire life.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.500
Possum was written and directed by Matthew&nbsp;
Holness, whose short story of the same name was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.360
originally published in the anthology collection&nbsp;
The New Uncanny: Tales of Unease. Horror writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:23.820
were prompted to use an essential human fear&nbsp;
and Sigmund Freud's essay on the uncanny as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:29.880
jumping off point. Holness ultimately drew&nbsp;
from not one but two phobias: puppets and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:35.340
doubles. Considering that the Possum puppet also&nbsp;
boasts some freakishly horrible spider legs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:41.520
we'll go ahead and unofficially count that as&nbsp;
three phobias. The result of this gumbo of fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:47.220
is some truly adrenaline-pumping nightmare&nbsp;
fuel about a very sad man trying to destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.840
something that's as much a representation of&nbsp;
his own trauma as a reflection of himself.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.720
"The black, long-legged Possum-man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.260 --> 00:09:00.653
Children, run! He'll eat and&nbsp;
smother, any child without a mother."

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:07.020
In the year 2000, the teenage Celeste is a victim&nbsp;
of a school shooting. Somehow, despite a serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:12.420
neck wound, she manages to survive, although the&nbsp;
same can't be said for all of her classmates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:17.100
In the wake of the tragedy, Celeste performs&nbsp;
at the memorial service an original song she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:22.200
wrote while in the hospital, which then sets&nbsp;
her on the path to international pop stardom.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:27.720
Vox Lux earns its place on this list thanks&nbsp;
to its alarming and emotionally devastating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:33.360
depiction of a school shooting. Considering&nbsp;
the rage-inducing reality of how frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.760
such tragedies occur in the United States,&nbsp;
the opening scene feels like a gut punch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:42.240
one where the effects linger&nbsp;
long after the credits roll.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:47.880
That Celeste builds a career off of that&nbsp;
horrific act of violence never sits quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:52.800
right. And it isn't until the film's final&nbsp;
moments that the uneasy feeling comes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:10:00.240
clearer focus. Vox Lux is one of the few films&nbsp;
in recent memory to truly consider the lasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.620
psychological and cultural aftershocks&nbsp;
of school shootings, from the genuine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:09.540
lasting pain they inflict on survivors&nbsp;
to the commodification of that very pain.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:17.160
Many films depict mental institutions as creepy&nbsp;
on the basis that older, better films told us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:23.280
they were creepy. But 2018's Unsane proves that&nbsp;
there's still plenty of substance to be found by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:28.020
shining a light on the institutional abuses&nbsp;
that continue to rage behind closed doors.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:33.480
Shot entirely on an iPhone 7 Plus,&nbsp;
Unsane breaks from the shuffling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:39.180
white-robed visual language of its modern&nbsp;
peers to underline the very real fears that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:42.960
still plague the modern psychiatric&nbsp;
experience. The story follows Sawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.860
a woman who unknowingly commits herself&nbsp;
to a mental institution during a routine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:52.380
visit to a therapist. Trapped in a situation&nbsp;
where she's treated as less than a person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:58.260
she's horrified to learn that her old stalker&nbsp;
works in the hospital as an orderly. But nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:03.540
believes that she has an active restraining order&nbsp;
against this man. Either that, or nobody cares.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:06.780
"I'm trying to tell you- you're not even&nbsp;
listening to me. I'm trying to tell you the man-"&nbsp;

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:09.900
"And I'm adding a course of&nbsp;
risperidone and lithium to your meds."

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.920
One of the more striking entries in&nbsp;
director Steven Soderbergh's ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:19.920
interest in recession-era anxiety, Unsane exposes&nbsp;
the greedy cost of the American healthcare system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.980
which is keen to harm healthy people if it means&nbsp;
a profit can be turned. Intentionally garish and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:29.760
ugly to look at, this film is an&nbsp;
unnerving viewing experience made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:33.180
all the creepier by its politically&nbsp;
charged approach to exploitation.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:39.780
2019's Swallow follows Haley Bennett as&nbsp;
Hunter, a newlywed former saleswoman who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:44.880
feels increasingly stifled by her extremely&nbsp;
comfortable and highly controlled domestic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:48.600
life. You might look at Hunter's life&nbsp;
and think she has it all. The loving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:53.520
husband. The lavish New York villa. A&nbsp;
baby on the way. So why is she so sad?

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:57.960
Under the increasingly crushing&nbsp;
pressure of her husband and in-laws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:04.320
Hunter's deep desire to be obedient and submissive&nbsp;
spills out in the form of a bizarre obsession:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:10.620
a compulsive desire to swallow inedible objects.&nbsp;
It's the only thing that makes her feel in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:16.860
control. But as Hunter's addiction with eating&nbsp;
hairpins, marbles, and tacks increasingly puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:23.280
her unborn child's viability at risk, her mental&nbsp;
health crisis reveals her husband's true colors.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:28.740
Easily one of the most squirm-inducing&nbsp;
viewing experiences in recent memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:34.980
Swallow is a haunting and isolating film about&nbsp;
the domestic imprisonment and abuse of power many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:39.720
women are forced to endure. Much of the film's&nbsp;
disturbing power comes from its sound design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:46.620
which emphasizes the visceral quality of Hunter's&nbsp;
digestive rebellion. The sound of teeth scraping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:54.240
across batteries is both unmistakable and&nbsp;
nauseating. There are plenty of movies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:58.320
are bloodier than Swallow, but not many&nbsp;
that are more difficult to sit through.

